  "pd": {
    "basis": "traditional",
    "detail": "Anonymous Appalachian dance song attached to a Clay County, Kentucky figure who died in 1885; in wide oral circulation among southern mountain fiddlers and singers through the late nineteenth century and carried across the country by Appalachian soldiers in 1917.",
    "sourceEdition": "Traditional two-strain old-time fiddle setting, the standard A-mixolydian version with the flat-seventh change, reduced to melody and changes",
    "notes": "Melody and harmony only, no lyrics. Encoded as the traditional common-stock two-strain tune, not any modern recorded arrangement. Written as a cut-time reel notated in 4/4; mode is major with the seventh degree flattened (acc -1), which is the mixolydian character of the tune and the source of the bVII chord. The two-note low pickup that opens the A strain is carried at the end of the preceding section."
  },
